{#
  Docker daemon configuration.

  live-restore keeps containers running while dockerd restarts. It matters here
  because the kubelet reaches containerd through the cri-dockerd shim, which
  means every cluster container is a Docker container: without this setting, a
  routine 'apt upgrade' that happens to pull a new Docker version stops every
  pod on the node. containerd itself needs no equivalent — its per-container
  shim processes survive a daemon restart by design.

  This defers the restart rather than removing it. Containers keep using the
  runtime they were started with, so an upgraded runtime does not apply to them
  (including its security fixes) until they are recreated — drain the node to
  do that at a time of your choosing. Deferring is the whole point: installing
  the update stops being the same event as disrupting the cluster.

  Docker supports live restore across patch-level daemon upgrades. A larger
  version jump may stop containers regardless, so it is not a guarantee.
#}
{
  "live-restore": {{ docker_live_restore | bool | to_json }}
}
